// Round 32 (R32-C, F3): the cancelled-group family
// R31-C's five shapes missed — a standing core date
// on the header line followed by groups carrying
// cancelled/canceled INSIDE the group, its sibling
// shapes (distinct-date cancelled groups, one group
// holding thousands of dates, leading groups before
// a relative core, bracket groups), each of them
// quadratic on the un-fixed tree. The rescans, now
// bounded: the bare test judged a phrase unit at
// every position of an occurrence's region,
// nothing-positions included, and each attempt
// rescanned the whole run of tokens that clean to
// nothing standing there (a de-grouped core is
// mostly the composition's own ";" markers); it
// also rebuilt the run's bracket-span mask once
// per occurrence. The target-claim test rescanned
// the whole token run per occurrence — the "to"
// walk-back, the phrase rebuild, the span/start
// questions — where one shared scan now states
// each answer once per run. The leading-group
// readers rebuilt the full balanced-span list of
// the remainder for every group stripped or
// judged; the first-span walk stops where the
// first span closes. The weekday fallback in the
// inner date reader joined and re-parsed the
// whole line suffix after every weekday word; a
// suffix holding more reader pieces than any date
// the flexible reader can read (three, plus one
// leading weekday) is now skipped, as the reader
// itself answers no date for it. And the by-date /
// distinct / alternatives membership tests in the
// group and plain compositions stand in sets
// beside their lists instead of scanning per
// occurrence. The readings on every shape below
// are the readings the un-fixed code produces
// (verified against a git-archive copy of the
// round-opening tree), pinned first at small size;
// the timing pins then hold each large shape to a
// bound at least five times the fixed code's
// measured debug time and at most a third of the
// un-fixed code's at the same size. Measurements:
// un-fixed, red team, release profile, min-of-5
// with the absolute-group control interleaved at
// the same group count — cancelled-with-core
// 11,605 ms at 8,000 groups (145x control, 3.2-4.1x
// per doubling), relative-cancelled 35,199 ms
// (447x), distinct-date cancelled 116x, one big
// group of same dates 188x, of distinct dates 36x,
// leading groups 32x, bracket groups ~11x at
// 608 KB and growing; un-fixed, this batch's debug
// probe, min-of-5 on the loaded VM (load average
// ~5.6), parse only — cancelled-with-core 4,915 ms
// at 2,000 groups and ~3.3x per doubling. Fixed,
// the same debug probe, min-of-5, same load: at
// 8,000 groups — cancelled absolute 3,987 ms,
// cancelled relative 2,617 ms, cancelled distinct
// 4,934 ms, one big group distinct 4,590 ms,
// leading 2,164 ms, bracket 1,052 ms, each ~2x per
// doubling; one big group of same dates 1,369 ms
// at 4,000, scaled linearly for its pin.
//
// Round 33 (R33-D, F4 = review MINOR-1): the
// sentence above the measurements does not hold
// on the numbers it cites, and the record is
// corrected here. The five-times half held (the
// absolute bounds stood at 5.0-5.7x the fixed
// measurements), but "at most a third of the
// un-fixed code's" was false on the recorded
// numbers: cancelled-with-core's un-fixed figure
// is 11,605 ms, a third of which is ~3.9 s
// against a 20 s bound, and relative-cancelled's
// is 35,199 ms, a third ~11.7 s against a 15 s
// bound. The frames were also mixed — the
// un-fixed figures are release, the fixed figures
// and the bounds debug — and the absolute bounds
// judged the machine as much as the code: the
// review lane watched the headline pin fail at
// 22.24 s against its 20 s bound in a contended
// run and pass at 19.77 s standalone, for a shape
// the fixed code parses in ~4 s. The pins below
// therefore carry no seconds bound at all. Each
// measures its witness and the absolute-group
// control (the standing core followed by 8,000
// plain "(November 1, 2026)" groups, the control
// of the round-32 measurements above) interleaved
// in the same run — three rounds of
// control-then-witness, the best time of each
// kept — and asserts the witness's best is under
// a per-shape multiple of the control's best.
// Load multiplies both sides of the ratio and
// cancels; the best of three discards the rounds
// a load spike lands in. The multiples and the
// windows they sit in were measured in this
// suite's own frame (debug, parse only, this VM)
// in this round. Fixed code, interleaved
// best-of-three at 8,000 groups, at load average
// ~6-9 with other work building on the box:
// cancelled absolute 6.7x control, cancelled
// relative 5.2x, cancelled distinct 5.7x, one big
// group of same dates 4.2x, of distinct dates
// 5.3x, leading groups 3.4x, bracket groups 1.7x;
// quieter probe runs (load ~2-3, best-of-three at
// 4,000 groups and single runs at 8,000) put the
// same ratios at 6.0-6.9x, 4.0-4.3x, 5.6-6.3x,
// 3.8-4.2x, 4.6-4.9x, 3.1-3.3x, 1.6x. The
// headline pair shows what the ratio buys: its
// absolute times inflated from 4,137 ms / 679 ms
// (best-of-three, load ~2) to 7,501 ms / 1,119 ms
// in the contended interleaved run, while the
// ratio moved only 6.1x to 6.7x. Un-fixed code
// (the round-opening tree, 31c6f90, measured in a
// scratch worktree in the same debug frame): at
// 8,000 groups, single runs at load ~3.5 —
// cancelled absolute 119,586 ms (124x the
// control's 966 ms), cancelled relative
// 250,167 ms (259x), cancelled distinct 76,796 ms
// (~79x), one big group of same dates 231,280 ms
// (239x), of distinct dates 17,931 ms (18.6x),
// leading groups 36,711 ms (38x), bracket groups
// 5,156 ms (5.3x); at 4,000 groups, best-of-three
// at load ~2-3.3, the same ratios stood at 61x,
// 158x, 59x, 169x, 8.6x, 16x, 2.7x. The bounds —
// 20x, 20x, 20x, 20x, 10x, 9x, 3x in the pins'
// order — sit between the fixed and un-fixed
// ratios on every shape at the pinned size, with
// at least ~1.7x of headroom on the fixed side
// and ~1.7x of catch on the un-fixed side; the
// bracket pin's window (fixed 1.7x, un-fixed
// 5.3x, bound 3x) and the big-distinct pin's
// (fixed 5.3x, un-fixed 18.6x, bound 10x) are the
// family's narrowest and are recorded as such.
// The release-frame ratios in the round-32
// measurements above are kept as provenance only;
// no bound below rests on them.

#[test]
fn r32c_cancelled_group_family_keeps_its_readings() {
    // Cancelled full-date groups after a standing
    // core: the core reads, the groups name nothing.
    let groups = ["(November 1, 2026, cancelled)"; 3].join(" ");
    let text = format!("For Sunday: November 1, 2026 {groups}\n\n## Announcement\n\nHi.\n");
    let c = parse_contribution(&text, today()).expect("parses");
    assert_eq!(c.for_date, Some(nov(1)));
    assert_eq!(c.for_sunday_parenthesized_dates, Vec::new());
    assert_eq!(c.for_sunday_second_dates, Vec::new());
    assert_eq!(week_verdict(&c, Some(nov(1))), WeekVerdict::SameSunday);
    assert_eq!(
        week_verdict(&c, Some(nov(8))),
        WeekVerdict::DifferentSunday(nov(1))
    );

    // Cancelled relative groups after the core read
    // the same way.
    let groups = ["(next Sunday, cancelled)"; 3].join(" ");
    let text = format!("For Sunday: November 1, 2026 {groups}\n\n## Announcement\n\nHi.\n");
    let c = parse_contribution(&text, today()).expect("parses");
    assert_eq!(c.for_date, Some(nov(1)));
    assert_eq!(c.for_sunday_in_words, None);
    assert_eq!(c.for_sunday_second_dates, Vec::new());
    assert_eq!(week_verdict(&c, Some(nov(1))), WeekVerdict::SameSunday);

    // Distinct-date cancelled groups after the core:
    // the core reads, the cancelled dates are named
    // nowhere.
    let text = "For Sunday: November 1, 2026 (November 2, 2026, cancelled) (November 3, 2026, cancelled) (November 4, 2026, cancelled)\n\n## Announcement\n\nHi.\n";
    let c = parse_contribution(text, today()).expect("parses");
    assert_eq!(c.for_date, Some(nov(1)));
    assert_eq!(c.for_sunday_second_dates, Vec::new());
    assert_eq!(week_verdict(&c, Some(nov(1))), WeekVerdict::SameSunday);

    // One big group holding the core's date three
    // times: the core reads, nothing else is named.
    let inner = "November 1, 2026, ".repeat(3);
    let text = format!("For Sunday: November 1, 2026 ({inner})\n\n## Announcement\n\nHi.\n");
    let c = parse_contribution(&text, today()).expect("parses");
    assert_eq!(c.for_date, Some(nov(1)));
    assert_eq!(c.for_sunday_second_dates, Vec::new());
    assert_eq!(week_verdict(&c, Some(nov(1))), WeekVerdict::SameSunday);

    // One big group holding three other dates: the
    // core reads and the group's dates are named as
    // second dates, in written order.
    let text = "For Sunday: November 1, 2026 (November 2, 2026, November 3, 2026, November 4, 2026)\n\n## Announcement\n\nHi.\n";
    let c = parse_contribution(text, today()).expect("parses");
    assert_eq!(c.for_date, Some(nov(1)));
    assert_eq!(c.for_sunday_second_dates, vec![nov(2), nov(3), nov(4)]);
    assert_eq!(week_verdict(&c, Some(nov(1))), WeekVerdict::SameSunday);

    // Leading groups before a relative core: the
    // phrase stands in words with the groups
    // remembered as its dates.
    let groups = ["(November 1, 2026)"; 3].join(" ");
    let text = format!("For Sunday: {groups} next Sunday\n\n## Announcement\n\nHi.\n");
    let c = parse_contribution(&text, today()).expect("parses");
    assert_eq!(c.for_date, None);
    assert_eq!(
        c.for_sunday_in_words.as_deref(),
        Some("(November 1, 2026) (November 1, 2026) (November 1, 2026) next Sunday")
    );
    assert_eq!(c.for_sunday_parenthesized_date, Some(nov(1)));
    assert_eq!(
        c.for_sunday_parenthesized_dates,
        vec![nov(1), nov(1), nov(1)]
    );
    assert_eq!(
        week_verdict(&c, Some(nov(1))),
        WeekVerdict::SundayInWords(nov(1))
    );

    // Bracket groups after the core: the core reads,
    // the bracketed dates name nothing.
    let groups = ["[November 1, 2026]"; 3].join(" ");
    let text = format!("For Sunday: November 1, 2026 {groups}\n\n## Announcement\n\nHi.\n");
    let c = parse_contribution(&text, today()).expect("parses");
    assert_eq!(c.for_date, Some(nov(1)));
    assert_eq!(c.for_sunday_second_dates, Vec::new());
    assert_eq!(week_verdict(&c, Some(nov(1))), WeekVerdict::SameSunday);

    // The Keep through line carries the cancelled
    // shapes the same way.
    let groups = ["(November 1, 2026, cancelled)"; 3].join(" ");
    let text = format!(
        "For Sunday: November 8, 2026\nKeep through: November 1, 2026 {groups}\n\n## Announcement\n\nHi.\n"
    );
    let c = parse_contribution(&text, today()).expect("parses");
    assert_eq!(c.for_date, Some(nov(8)));
    assert_eq!(c.keep_through, Some(nov(1)));

    let groups = ["(next Sunday, cancelled)"; 3].join(" ");
    let text = format!(
        "For Sunday: November 8, 2026\nKeep through: November 1, 2026 {groups}\n\n## Announcement\n\nHi.\n"
    );
    let c = parse_contribution(&text, today()).expect("parses");
    assert_eq!(c.keep_through, Some(nov(1)));
}

// Round 33 (R33-D, F4): the timing pins' shared
// measurement. The control is the absolute-group
// control of the pin block's header: the standing
// core followed by 8,000 plain groups, the shape
// that stood at control cost on both the fixed and
// the un-fixed tree. Witness and control are parsed
// interleaved, three rounds of control-then-witness,
// and the best time of each is returned beside the
// witness's last parse, so a pin judges the ratio
// between them, never a wall-clock bound (see the
// header for the measured windows each pin's
// multiple sits in).
fn r33d_absolute_group_control() -> String {
    let groups = vec!["(November 1, 2026)"; 8000].join(" ");
    format!("For Sunday: November 1, 2026 {groups}\n\n## Announcement\n\nHi.\n")
}

fn r33d_interleaved_best(
    witness: &str,
    control: &str,
) -> (Contribution, std::time::Duration, std::time::Duration) {
    let mut witness_best = std::time::Duration::MAX;
    let mut control_best = std::time::Duration::MAX;
    let mut parsed = None;
    for _ in 0..3 {
        let start = std::time::Instant::now();
        parse_contribution(control, today()).expect("the control parses");
        control_best = control_best.min(start.elapsed());
        let start = std::time::Instant::now();
        let c = parse_contribution(witness, today()).expect("parses");
        witness_best = witness_best.min(start.elapsed());
        parsed = Some(c);
    }
    (
        parsed.expect("three rounds ran"),
        witness_best,
        control_best,
    )
}

// Round 34 (R34-D, F7 = red team RT34-7): the pins
// below sampled exactly one size (8,000 groups), so
// a genuinely quadratic term with a small coefficient
// passed them while dominating at sizes a
// contribution line can actually carry. Each pin now
// also measures at a second size, 16,000 groups —
// one doubling — in the same interleaved best-of-3
// ratio form, against the same per-shape multiple:
// the 8,000-group bounds and every number recorded
// in the pin block's header above are untouched. A
// term quadratic in the group count contributes
// twice as much, relative to the linear control, at
// the doubled size as at the pinned size, so a term
// that passes at 8,000 can fail at 16,000: the red
// team's worked example, a term contributing 12x of
// control at 8,000 groups (total ~19x, under the
// loosest pins' 20x), contributes 24x at 16,000 for a
// total of ~30x and fails. Fixed code, interleaved
// best-of-three at 16,000 groups, measured in this
// suite's own frame (debug, parse only, this VM) at
// load average ~1.3: cancelled absolute 5.98x
// control, cancelled relative 4.95x, cancelled
// distinct 5.30x, one big group of same dates 3.87x,
// of distinct dates 4.85x, leading groups 3.24x,
// bracket groups 1.66x — the 8,000-group ratios
// (6.7x, 5.2x, 5.7x, 4.2x, 5.3x, 3.4x, 1.7x) standing
// flat at the doubled size, as linear code's ratios
// should. The second-size bounds are the pins' own
// multiples (20x, 20x, 20x, 20x, 10x, 9x, 3x), which
// sit at least ~1.8x above the fixed ratios measured
// here and below what the worked-example term
// reaches on every shape.
fn r34d_absolute_group_control_at(groups_n: usize) -> String {
    let groups = vec!["(November 1, 2026)"; groups_n].join(" ");
    format!("For Sunday: November 1, 2026 {groups}\n\n## Announcement\n\nHi.\n")
}

// Round 39 (R39-CI): the r31c pins' shared control
// for their ratio form (see the correction note at
// the r31c pin block's header). The r32c family's
// control — plain groups after a standing core —
// cannot serve as this family's linear baseline:
// on the tree before R31-C that shape carried the
// same cliff as the witnesses (this round's
// single-run measurements on that tree, 20b5aed:
// the plain-group control at 32,000 groups took
// 169.0 s against the semicolon witness's 93.7 s,
// and the full-date witness against the 8,000-group
// control stood at a ratio of 6.99 against its
// fixed-tree 4.98 — no multiple sits in that
// window). The control here is the R30-D shape —
// the core date repeated bare on the For line —
// whose cost lives in the prefix reader the R31-C
// fix never touched, so it reads at the same cost
// on both sides of that fix and the ratios judge
// the group machinery, not the machine. At 8,000
// repetitions it stands in the r32c control's cost
// class (best-of-three ≈ 2 s in this suite's
// frame).
fn r39ci_repeated_date_control() -> String {
    let line = vec!["November 1, 2026"; 8000].join(", ");
    format!("For Sunday: {line}\n\n## Announcement\n\nHi.\n")
}

